\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{What a fermionic transfer theorem must supply}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{A positive determinant gives the ordinary connection law #{\nu} of \ref{fdip-00IP}. It does not establish positivity or reflection positivity of the full Grassmann functional, a physical fermion Hilbert space, or electron time evolution. Those conclusions require a fermionic transfer construction with its actual spin projectors, adjoint and reflection conventions, boundary conditions, gauge constraint and observable correspondence.}

\p{Lüscher supplies such a construction for his four-dimensional Wilson #{SU(N)} theory \citet{DESY 76/54 preprint, printed p. 1}{luscher1977transfer}. In the DESY 76/54 preprint, printed pp. 6–9, the spatial matrix #{B} in (13) satisfies #{B\geq1-6|\kappa|}, and the transfer construction uses #{0<\kappa<1/6}. Proposition 1 gives its bounded self-adjoint, gauge-invariant, strictly positive transfer operator. Printed pp. 10–11, (28) and Proposition 2, identify time-ordered Euclidean insertions with normally ordered operators under periodic spatial and gauge-time boundaries and anticyclic fermion-time boundaries. See \citet{DESY 76/54 preprint, printed pp. 6-11, Propositions 1-2}{luscher1977transfer}. The finite-periodic temporal-gauge qualification in footnote 3, printed p. 16, remains part of that comparison \citet{DESY 76/54 preprint, footnote 3, printed p. 16}{luscher1977transfer}. These are the hypotheses behind [[fdip-0087]].}

\p{\ref{fdip-00JA} checks its numerical inequality in dimension #{4} with #{r=1}; it does not identify the full transfer construction. The present class allows every lattice dimension #{d\geq1} with a supplied graded spin module, all positive periods, all #{c_W\geq0}, periodic fermions in every direction and group #{U(1)}. The source fixes different group and temporal-boundary data, as well as its spinor realization and normal ordering. Matching its transfer kernel to this class would require an explicit proof of the Abelian adaptation and the boundary/observable correspondence. None follows from the determinant sign or from a bosonic transfer theorem. The determinant, source, Ward and dressed-observable identities give finite Abelian charged-matter mathematics. Fermionic quantum reconstruction for this class, and a continuum QED limit, remain unproved.}
